\section{Abstract}
Opioid abuse disorders are one of the most pressing public health challenges in the United States. So much so, the issue obtained the title “The Opioid Crisis”. Recent years have shown severe impacts in states such as North Carolina. This project investigates the predictive power of Social Determinants of Health (SDoH), in modeling opioid-related mortality rates, separated by county. We chose both linear and polynomial regression models on the 2018–2022 crude death rates, to predict 2023 rates and compared their performance against a regression model based on a Vulnerability Index derived from principal component analysis (PCA) of the key SDoH indicators. When comparing approaches, the linear regression model for the crude rates had the lowest root mean-squared error on the test data, suggesting that neither higher-degree polynomial models, nor the PCA of SDoH model outperformed the simpler linear approach under current data restrictions. We then discuss possible sources of error, including data suppression and regional reporting limitations, and state that future work might focus on range-based predictions and alternative model types.

\section{Introduction}
Substance use disorder (SUD) remains a critical public health crisis in the United States, with opioid use disorder (OUD) proving to be one of the greatest challenges. In 2022, approximately 76\% of drug overdose deaths throughout the country involved opioids. The number of opioid overdoses has increased tenfold since 1999 [1]. Despite the CDC's recent report of an almost 24\% decline in drug overdose deaths in 2024 [2], combating this crisis continues to be a serious public health concern. This report focuses specifically on the state of North Carolina, which has one of the highest opioid misuse rates in the country. \\
Several techniques have been proposed to model this crisis or issues, including [3], [4].  The unique challenge in the modeling of public health issues is the effect of external circumstances. Factors beyond direct medical care affect the health and well-being of a population. 
Our study aims to investigate the role of Social Determinants of Health (SDoH) in the spread of opioid overdose-related deaths. As described by the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, SDoH fall into five main categories:
\begin{itemize}
\item Economic Stability
\item Education Access and Quality
\item Health Care Access and Quality
\item Neighborhood and Built Environment
\item Social and Community Context
\end{itemize}
These variables can provide proper context to the crisis of opioid overdoses. The purpose of this project is to investigate the extent to which Social Determinants of Health can be used to predict negative opioid outcomes at the county level in North Carolina. The creation of a Vulnerability Index helps public health officials identify regions at increased risk for opioid mortality and abuse, allowing for proper allocation of scarce resources such as treatment centers and Naloxone.

\section{Data}
Data for this report was pulled from the following sites:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Source: CDC Wonder Current Multiple Cause of Death Data
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item Coverage: United States 2018 - 2023
