\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Linear and Polynomial Regression Models for CDC Data}
Our study used linear and polynomial regression modeling methods to construct predictive models for future opioid-related death rates. The descriptive variables were the past five years of data for each county from the CDC Wonder dataset: 
\begin{align*}
    x_1: &\qquad  \text{crude rate from 1 year ago}\\
    x_2: &\qquad  \text{crude rate from 2 years ago}\\
    x_3: &\qquad  \text{crude rate from 3 years ago}\\
    x_4: &\qquad  \text{crude rate from 4 years ago}\\
    x_5: &\qquad  \text{crude rate from 5 years ago.}
\end{align*}
We constructed a matrix $X$ where the columns represent crude rate data from each year from 2018 to 2022 and the rows represent counties. The vector $y$ represents the crude rate data for 2023. The data was randomly split into training data (80\%) and testing data (20\%) to create a linear regression model ($p=1$) and polynomial regression models ($p = 2,3,4, \text{ and } 8$). We removed unreliable and suppressed data so that we had all numerical values with which to train and test the models.\\
