Following this model, we found four indicator variables to be statistically significant: \textbf{Renter occupied: 1.51 or more occupants per room, Rate: No Health Insurance, Percent Some College} and \textbf{Percent Unemployed}. The variables were selected as County Descriptors. 
\newline In order to assign each county with an individual Vulnerability Index, we ran PCA to project this four-dimensional data onto a one-dimensional subspace. From our previous regression, we found the Percent Some College and Renter occupied: 1.51 or more occupants per room were negatively associated with Crude Rate. As a result, we multiplied each column by $-1$ before applying PCA. We then scaled each selected column to have a mean of $0$, and a standard deviation of $1$. We define the Vulnerability Index as
\[
\tilde{V} = X \cdot v
\]
where X is the column vector of descriptor data for each county and $v$ is the principal component vector. The Vulnerability Indexes with more negative values indicating higher vulnerability to negative opioid outcomes. For a more interpretable metric, we also calculated a Vulnerability Score using max-min normalization. This score ranges from $0$ to $100$, with higher values indicating higher vulnerability to higher opioid overdose rates. The following equation shows the normalization
\[
\text{Vulnerability Score}_i = \left( \frac{\tilde{V}_i - \min(\tilde{V})}{\max(\tilde{V}) - \min(\tilde{V})} \right) \times 100.
\]
In order to determine our prediction abilities, we split our Crude Rate data into testing and training subsets using \begin{minted}{python}
    sklearn.model_selection.
\end{minted}
80\% of the data was used to train a linear regression mode, and 20\% was left to test. We fit a new linear model using only the Vulnerability Score as an explanatory variable and found the equation.
\[
y = 0.4482x + 8.4829.
\]
We finally calculated the room mean-squared error of the testing data to quantify our ability to predict Crude Rate.


\section{Results}
Comparing the polynomial models, we found that $p=1$ gives the best prediction with a calculated root means-squared error of $4.44$. We found that the linear model using Vulnerability Index as the predictor variable had a calculated root means-squared error of $13.08$. Table 3 shows the root MSE for each model.
\begin{table}[h!]
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{lrr}
        \hline
        \textbf{Past Years' Crude Rate Model} & \textbf{Degree} & \textbf{Root MSE} \\
        \hline
        Linear Regression & $p=1$ & 3.93 \\
        Polynomial Regression & $p=2$ &  114.75 \\
        Polynomial Regression & $p=3$ & 131.87 \\
        Polynomial Regression & $p=4$ & 801.24 \\
        Polynomial Regression & $p=8$ & 229830.19 \\
        \hline
        \textbf{Vulnerability Index Model} & \textbf{Degree} & \textbf{Root MSE} \\
        \hline
        Linear Regression & $p=1$ & 13.08\\ 
        \hline
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Regression Models and their Root Means-Squared Errors}
    \label{tab:my_label}
\end{table}
\newline In the vulnerability index model, the average prediction error is approximately 13 opioid overdose deaths per 10{,}000 total deaths. This is a substantial margin of error, particularly when considered in the context of North Carolina counties, where most observed crude overdose mortality rates fall between 15 and 45 deaths per 10{,}000. As such, this level of predictive inaccuracy constitutes a significant proportion of the total outcome range and limits the model’s effectiveness for precise forecasting or targeted intervention planning.
\\
If we treat the linear regression crude rate model as the ``standard" against which we compare the vulnerability index model, we cannot conclude that Social Determinants of Health (SDoH) data improves our prediction of opioid-related death rates.

