\section{Discussion}
Our initial goal was to construct a predictive model for opioid crude mortality using county-level social determinants of health data to see if these factors could improve predictions for opioid-related deaths. However, we encountered substantial challenges. Below, we address some of these challenges and offer possible avenues for further research queries.
\begin{description}
    \item[Limited Data:] A possible source of the large error in all of the models is that many counties have suppressed or missing data. In particular, low-population and rural areas. This means that for the models based on past years' crude rates, splitting that data into 80\% training data and 20\% testing data only gives seven test points after removing the ``Unreliable" and ``Suppressed" data.
    \item[Inaccuracy of the ``Standard" Model:] To determine if the model based on SDoH data improved predictions, we constructed a ``standard" model based only on prior crude rate data and compared the root MSEs of the models. We recognize that this model is by no means a perfect standard for prediction models, as is evident from a root MSE of almost four, and we recognize that there may be other models that could be used for comparison.
    \item[Other Variables Not Considered:] Our study utilized only four indicator variables in order to predict opioid mortality. In reality, a surplus of other factors are significant in making a county or area more vulnerable to opioid mortality. Future studies may consider opioid dispensing rates, Naloxone administration, as well as sociodemographic data in order to improve forecasting.
    \item[Establishing Ranges Instead of Exact Crude Rates:] Due to the variability of human behavior, an investigation into constructing ranges for crude death rates may be more pertinent to the subject matter. Instead of trying to predict exact values, a study that aims to establish ranges may provide better estimates for resources needed to combat opioid misuse in each area. Another possible approach would be to establish ranges that assign a vulnerability rating (high, medium, low) to certain values.
\end{description}

\section{Conclusion}
After creating and comparing predictive models for crude rates of opioid-related deaths, we did not find that Social Determinants of Health data contributes to improved accuracy in crude rate predictions. Nevertheless, our findings are meaningful as they illustrate the challenges of working with real-world public health data. In particular, we note the unique issues of needing more granular data when privacy is a large concern. We conclude that predictive modeling on this scale is non-trivial and might benefit from improved data collection and reporting.

